{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "NSE Bhavcopy Portfolio Lookup — Connect Prices to Holdings",
  "scenario": "You manage a small portfolio of 10 Nifty stocks. NSE's daily bhavcopy (price feed) arrived this morning. Your Portfolio sheet lists your holdings by ticker symbol; the Bhavcopy sheet contains today's OHLCV data. You need to pull live closing prices, calculate portfolio values, and identify which stocks are missing from today's bhavcopy — all using lookup functions.",
  "instructions": "Build VLOOKUP, INDEX-MATCH, and XLOOKUP formulas to connect the Portfolio sheet to the Bhavcopy data, then calculate daily portfolio metrics.",
  "learning_objective": "Apply VLOOKUP with pinned ranges and exact-match logic, construct INDEX-MATCH two-way lookups, and deploy XLOOKUP with graceful error handling to join NSE price feeds to holdings — and explain why INDEX-MATCH outlives VLOOKUP in production models.",
  "hints": [
    "For VLOOKUP, remember: pin the table array with dollar signs ($A$2:$E$11), omit the fourth argument and it defaults to approximate, and always state 0 or FALSE for exact match on ticker names.",
    "MATCH returns a position (1, 2, 3…); INDEX uses that position to fetch a value from any column of your choice — MATCH finds the row, INDEX reads it. Try nesting MATCH inside INDEX.",
    "XLOOKUP syntax is =XLOOKUP(lookup_value, lookup_array, return_array, [if_not_found]) — the return_array is independent of lookup_array, so you can look right, left, or anywhere. If you see #NAME?, your Excel version doesn't have XLOOKUP (pre-2021); use INDEX-MATCH instead."
  ],
